[moddesc   {Styles for Tk}]
[titledesc {Changeable look for Tk applications}]
[require Tk]
[require style [opt 0.3]]
[require style::as [opt 1.4.1]]
[require style::lobster [opt 0.2]]
[description]

The [package style] module changes the look of the classic Tk widgets
(not the themed [cmd ttk] widgets).  A style adds entries to the option
database, so that it applies to widgets created after it is set up, and
may create named fonts and bindings.

[para]

Two styles are available: [const as] (package [package style::as]) and
[const lobster] (package [package style::lobster]).  Using a style does
not undo the changes made by a style used before.

[section {COMMANDS}]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd style::names]]

Returns the list of the names of the available styles, [const lobster]
and [const as].

[call [cmd style::use] [arg style] [opt [arg "arg ..."]]]

Loads the package of the style ([package style::as] or
[package style::lobster]) and calls its [cmd init] command with the
given arguments, i.e. it is the same as

[example {
package require style::$style
style::${style}::init ?arg ...?
}]

[list_end]

[section {THE AS STYLE}]

The package [package style::as] provides a style which uses the colors
of the system on Windows and white widgets with a blue selection
elsewhere, smaller borders and fonts (Tahoma on Windows, Helvetica on
X11, Lucida Grande on macOS), and a mouse wheel binding which scrolls
the widget under the pointer.  It consists of the following parts:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const button]] the look of buttons.
[def [const entry]] the look of entries.
[def [const fonts]] the fonts of the classic widgets.
[def [const listbox]] the look of listboxes.
[def [const menu]] the look of menus.
[def [const menubutton]] the look of menubuttons.
[def [const misc]] the look of some widgets of other widget sets.
[def [const mousewheel]] the mouse wheel bindings described below.
[def [const panedwindow]] the look of panedwindows.
[def [const scrollbar]] the look of scrollbars.
[def [const spinbox]] the look of spinboxes.
[def [const text]] the look of text widgets.
[list_end]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd style::as::init] [opt "[option -priority] [arg priority]"] [opt [arg "part ..."]]]

Sets up the given parts of the style, or all of them if no part is
given.  The option database entries are added with the given
[arg priority] (see [cmd option]); the default is
[const widgetDefault].  The priority is remembered for later calls.

[call [cmd style::as::reset] [opt [arg "part ..."]]]

Undoes the given parts of the style, or all of them if no part is
given.  Only the [const mousewheel] part can be undone; the option
database entries are not removed.

[call [cmd style::as::enable] [arg what] [opt [arg "arg ..."]]]
[call [cmd style::as::disable] [arg what]]

Enables or disables a feature.  [arg what] is one of:

[list_begin definitions]

[def [const mousewheel]]

The same as [cmd {style::as::init mousewheel}] and
[cmd {style::as::reset mousewheel}].

[def "[const control-mousewheel] [arg type]"]

Turning the mouse wheel with the Control key pressed (also
Control-plus and Control-minus, Command on macOS) makes the font of
the widget under the pointer larger or smaller, within a limited
range.  It works only for widgets which use the fonts of the style,
that is, classic Tk widgets created after the [const fonts] part was
set up and not given a [option -font] of their own; it does not work
for widgets created before, or for [cmd ttk] widgets.  [arg type] is
[const local] to change the font of that widget only, or
[const global] to change the fonts of the style, and so of all
widgets which use them.

[list_end]

An error is raised for any other value of [arg what].

[list_end]

[subsection Fonts]

The [const fonts] part creates named fonts for the style and adds
them to the option database, so that the classic Tk widgets created
afterwards use them: a fixed-width font for text widgets and a
proportional font for the others.  The fonts are created only once,
so the part can be set up again.  An application can get the font of
the style for its own use from such a widget, e.g. with
[cmd {[.t cget -font]}].

[subsection {Mouse wheel}]

The [const mousewheel] part binds [const <MouseWheel>] and
[const <Shift-MouseWheel>] (also [const <Button-4>] and
[const <Button-5>] on X11) on [const all], so that the wheel scrolls
the widget under the pointer, or the widget with the focus if the
pointer is outside the application, vertically or, with Shift,
horizontally.  The class bindings of text and listbox widgets (and of
some widgets of other widget sets) for these events are removed so
that they do not scroll twice.  Widgets with
their own binding for the event are not scrolled.

[section {THE LOBSTER STYLE}]

The package [package style::lobster] provides a style that looks like
GTK applications ("gtklook" on the Tcl'ers wiki).  It has effect on
X11 only.

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd style::lobster::init] [opt "[option -priority] [arg priority]"]]

Adds the option database entries of the style, with the given
[arg priority] (default [const widgetDefault]), and creates the fonts
it uses.

[list_end]

[section EXAMPLE]

[example {
package require style
style::use as
style::as::enable control-mousewheel local
text .t
pack .t
}]

